  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Differentiate each function then determine whether the curve is increasing, decreasing, or stationary at $x=2$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p16-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y=\\dfrac{x}{x+1}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}$; $y'(2)=\\dfrac19$, so the curve is increasing.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{(x+1)(1)-x(1)}{(x+1)^2}\\\\y'&=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^2}\\\\y'(2)&=\\frac{1}{(2+1)^2}\\\\y'(2)&=\\frac19>0\\end{align*}$$\nThe curve is increasing at $x=2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 39
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y=\\dfrac{x+1}{x-1}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=-\\dfrac{2}{(x-1)^2}$; $y'(2)=-2$, so the curve is decreasing.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{(x-1)(1)-(x+1)(1)}{(x-1)^2}\\\\y'&=-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^2}\\\\y'(2)&=-\\frac{2}{(2-1)^2}\\\\y'(2)&=-2<0\\end{align*}$$\nThe curve is decreasing at $x=2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 39
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y=\\dfrac{x^2}{x+2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=\\dfrac{x^2+4x}{(x+2)^2}$; $y'(2)=\\dfrac34$, so the curve is increasing.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{(x+2)(2x)-x^2(1)}{(x+2)^2}\\\\y'&=\\frac{2x^2+4x-x^2}{(x+2)^2}\\\\y'&=\\frac{x^2+4x}{(x+2)^2}\\\\y'(2)&=\\frac{2^2+4(2)}{(2+2)^2}\\\\y'(2)&=\\frac{12}{16}\\\\y'(2)&=\\frac34>0\\end{align*}$$\nThe curve is increasing at $x=2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 47
      }
    ]
  },
